{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788364166,"nonce":"87hja6u2rT/vn1267zeCT7YE9J0=","message":"**SHIPPED, worker, repair round session 22.** Branch `fix/b7359fa-designate-reach`, commit `e07347a` (rebased onto `main` at `3b5c431` after the f9dadc7/e08c3e5 merge landed mid-round; the branch now contains `main` and merges clean — the only conflict was DESIGN.md's tail, both sides kept).\n\n**The orchestrator's resolution was taken as given and not re-derived.** All three facts are load-bearing in the code and each is cited at the line that depends on it.\n\n\n`designate` gains `accepted_actionable` and `accepted_unreachable` beside `accepted`, plus a `reach` block:\n\n```\n\"reach\": {\n  \"applies\": true,\n  \"gate\": \"RimWorld/ForbidUtility.InAllowedArea(IntVec3, Pawn) — per pawn: playerSettings.EffectiveAreaRestrictionInPawnCurrentMap, ignored when TrueCount == 0\",\n  \"test\": \"ALLOWED AREA ONLY. This is not a pathing test…\",\n  \"roster\": \"map.mapPawns.FreeColonistsSpawned\",\n  \"work_type\": \"Hunting\",\n  \"work_source\": \"WorkGiverDef 'HunterHunt' (giverClass RimWorld.WorkGiver_HunterHunt) -\u003e workType Hunting\",\n  \"capable\": 4, \"enabled\": 2, \"unrestricted\": 0,\n  \"considered\": 6, \"actionable\": 0, \"unreachable\": 6,\n  \"pawns\":  [{pawn, name, enabled, priority, downed, area, area_id, area_cells, restricted, can_work}],\n  \"areas\":  [{id, label, cells, pawns, excludes}],\n  \"unreachable_targets\": [{at, id, def, label}], \"unreachable_more\": 0,\n  \"warning\": \"6 of 6 target(s) lie outside EVERY capable colonist's allowed area…\"\n}\n```\n\nAn **all-unreachable** batch is refused before anything is written, with `refused.{code, reason, hint}` and `code` one of `outside-every-allowed-area` / `no-capable-pawn`. A **mixed** batch reports and proceeds. The split also rides on the journal row's `counts`, so a post-mortem reads it without the envelope.\n\n\n1. **Per target, over the capable roster.** `DesignateReach.Score` calls `cell.InAllowedArea(pawn)` — the game's own extension method, per `PawnEmergencyVerbs.cs:161`'s form — for every (target, capable pawn). The cached `Area` on each roster row is used for LABELS and the per-area rollup only, never for the decision.\n2. **`accepted` is split, and the area is named.** `reach.areas[]` carries the area id, label, cell count, which pawns are bound to it, and **how many of the considered targets it excludes** — so \"which area excludes the rest\" is one field, not an inference. Refusal for all-unreachable; report for mixed, per the `place-layout` precedent you cited.\n3. **The gate is called, not re-derived**, and cited at the call site and in `reach.gate`.\n4. **`checklists/turn.md` §materials-designation-loop** gains an `also:` naming `accepted_unreachable`, `reach.enabled` and `composition` — amended once, in the `855117a` commit, because both issues rewrite that paragraph. The playbook lesson is rewritten to name the fields and is marked **half graduated to CODE** in `playbook/INDEX.md`.\n5. **`accept/b7359fa-designate-reach.py`**, 6 phases.\n\n\n- **The franchise is CAPABILITY (`!WorkTypeIsDisabled`), not assignment.** A capable colonist with the work switched off is one `work-priorities` call away; an incapable one never will be. Refusing over priority would refuse the wrong thing. So `enabled` is published beside `capable` and raises a `warning` when it is zero — which is C2's *other* half (128 designated `MineableSteel` cells, never mined) and there is nowhere else the agent sees it at the moment it designates.\n- **The refusal has a door: `allow_unreachable:true`.** Painting ahead of an area expansion is a thing players do, and a wall with no door is worse than a loud report. Same idiom as `posture`'s `allow_empty_area`.\n- **The designation → work type link is the game's own data.** The designator table names the `WorkGiver_*` CLASS (every entry verified in the decompile by member name); `DesignateReach.WorkTypeFor` resolves it through `DefDatabase\u003cWorkGiverDef\u003e` on `giverClass` and reads `workType`. A mod that re-homes a giver is honoured for free; a class no def claims yields `applies:false` **with a reason** rather than a guess. `Mine` and `MineVein` share `WorkGiver_Miner` — `MineAIUtility.PotentialMineables` unions both designations.\n\n\n**`accepted` is not the count of designations created.** `Designator_MineVein.DesignateSingleCell` flood-fills, so one accepted cell can paint forty and every later cell in the drag comes back already-designated and REJECTED. A reach report keyed on `accepted` would say \"1\" about a forty-cell job. `DesignateEngine.Landed` is new: the designation DELTA (cell snapshot before/after) for a cell-targeted def, the accepted things otherwise, with `designated_from` naming which reading the caller got.\n\n\n**Pathing.** A cell inside a pawn's area can still be unroutable. Naming the field `reach` and quietly meaning \"area\" would have been the same class of defect this issue is about, so `reach.test` states the limit. Filed as **`1d381be`** with the cost analysis and the `Reachability.CanReach` cache-write question a worker has to settle first.\n\n**The standing re-check.** Nothing re-evaluates designations already on the map when the area, the roster or the target moves — and in the incident that killed `Marco` the herd walked AFTER the designation was placed, so even this fix would have reported `actionable: 6` and been right at the time. Filed as **`9c68756`**; it is what the lesson's `Retire when` now names.\n\n\n```\npython3 accept/b7359fa-designate-reach.py            # 6 phases, needs the bench PAUSED\npython3 accept/b7359fa-designate-reach.py --selftest # offline, no bench\n```\n\n`--selftest` here, on the rebased tree: **all 27 self-checks passed** — including that its own `shape()` FAILS on an absent key and that `eq(…, None)` would have passed on it.\n\nPhase 2 is the one that proves the shape rather than the behaviour: it binds the whole roster to an area that excludes the fixture (refusal), then binds **one** colonist to a covering area and asserts the same batch becomes actionable. **A check written against a single colony-wide area cannot pass phase 2.** Phase 4 binds the roster to a `TrueCount == 0` area and asserts `restricted:false` with nothing unreachable.\n\n**THIS SUITE REBINDS EVERY COLONIST'S ALLOWED AREA.** The teardown restores each pawn's original area (recorded in phase 0 from `posture`'s read-only form) and runs even on failure — and phase 0 **aborts** rather than proceed if `posture` will not answer, because the suite must be able to put the colony back. Run it on the bench.\n\n`RIMWORLD_MANAGED=… dotnet build -c Release` clean, 0 warnings, on the rebased tree. **No assembly committed** — source only.","files":null}]}